      <h1>Introduction to Prompt <span class="accent">Engineering</span></h1>
      <p class="hero-sub">Master the art of communicating with language models. Learn to write effective prompts, understand model behavior, and develop strategies for getting the best responses from LLMs through careful prompt design and iterative refinement.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="objectives">
      <h4>Learning Objectives</h4>
      <ul>
        <li>Understand what prompt engineering is and why it matters</li>
        <li>Learn fundamental principles for effective prompts</li>
        <li>Write clear instructions that guide model responses</li>
        <li>Use context and constraints to shape model behavior</li>
        <li>Implement prompts with Python OpenAI library</li>
        <li>Iterate and refine prompts based on results</li>
        <li>Handle edge cases and unexpected model responses</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <section class="section" id="prompting-fundamentals">
      <span class="section-label">Fundamentals</span>
      <h2>What is Prompt Engineering?</h2>
      <p>Prompt engineering is the practice of designing input text (prompts) to guide language model responses toward desired outcomes. Unlike traditional programming where you write explicit instructions, prompt engineering works with probabilistic models that generate text token by token. The quality of your prompt directly affects response quality, relevance, and accuracy. Effective prompt engineering combines clear instructions, context, examples, and constraints to maximize model performance.</p>

      <p>Prompt engineering is not magic—it's a systematic approach to communicating with models. Different models respond differently to the same prompts, so testing and iteration are essential. Key principles include clarity (be specific), context (provide relevant information), examples (show desired format), and constraints (specify what to avoid).</p>

      <div class="key-concept">
        <strong>Key Concept:</strong> Prompt engineering is the primary interface to LLMs. A 10% improvement in prompts can outperform complex fine-tuning. Understanding how models interpret language is crucial for effective AI applications.
      </div>

      <h3>Basic Prompting with OpenAI API</h3>
      <p>The OpenAI API is the standard interface for accessing GPT models. You send a prompt and receive generated text. The API includes parameters like temperature (creativity), max_tokens (length), and top_p (diversity) that control response behavior.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="kw">import</span> <span class="va">openai</span>

<span class="va">openai</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">api_key</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"your-api-key-here"</span>

<span class="cm"># Simple prompt</span>
<span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">openai</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">ChatCompletion</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">create</span>(
    <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
    <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[
        {<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>, <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="st">"Explain quantum computing in 50 words"</span>}
    ],
    <span class="va">temperature</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">0.7</span>,  <span class="cm"># 0=deterministic, 1=creative</span>
    <span class="va">max_tokens</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="nm">100</span>
)

<span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">response</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">choices</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">message</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">content</span>)</span></code></pre>

      <h3>Clarity and Specificity in Prompts</h3>
      <p>Vague prompts produce vague results. Be specific about what you want: the format, style, length, and intended audience. Include constraints and edge cases. The more guidance you provide, the better the model can align with your goals.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Vague prompt (poor)</span>
<span class="va">prompt1</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"Tell me about Python"</span>

<span class="cm"># Specific prompt (better)</span>
<span class="va">prompt2</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""Write a Python function that:
- Takes a list of numbers as input
- Returns a dictionary with keys 'sum', 'mean', 'max', 'min'
- Handles empty lists by returning None for each metric
- Includes a docstring explaining parameters and return value
Include example usage."""</span>

<span class="cm"># Even better: with format specification</span>
<span class="va">prompt3</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""Write a Python function following these requirements:
REQUIREMENTS:
- Input: list of numbers (can be empty)
- Output: dict with keys 'sum', 'mean', 'max', 'min'
- Edge case: empty list returns {sum: None, mean: None, max: None, min: None}
- Include comprehensive docstring
- Add 2 example usages in a if __name__ == '__main__': block
STYLE: Use type hints, follow PEP 8"""</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="prompt-structure">
      <span class="section-label">Prompt Design</span>
      <h2>Structuring Effective Prompts</h2>
      <p>Well-structured prompts follow a consistent pattern: role/context, task, constraints, and format. This structure helps models understand your intent. Provide examples when possible (few-shot prompting). Explicitly state what you don't want to prevent common mistakes.</p>

      <h3>The RCFC Framework</h3>
      <p>Role (what the model should act as), Context (background information), Format (desired output structure), Constraints (limitations and rules). This structure provides maximum guidance to the model.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># RCFC Framework Example</span>

<span class="va">prompt</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="st">"""
ROLE: You are a Python expert code reviewer with 10+ years of experience.

CONTEXT: I'm building a machine learning pipeline and have written this data preprocessing function. I need feedback on code quality, performance, and best practices.

TASK: Review the following function and provide constructive feedback.

CONSTRAINTS:
- Focus on: readability, efficiency, error handling, and pythonic style
- Do NOT suggest rewriting in another language
- Assume NumPy and Pandas are available
- Must handle edge cases like empty dataframes

FORMAT: Provide feedback as:
1. Overall assessment (1-2 sentences)
2. 3-5 specific issues (each with: issue, why it matters, fix)
3. 2-3 things done well
4. Improved version of critical parts

FUNCTION CODE:
[insert code here]
"""</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="iterative-refinement">
      <span class="section-label">Practical Techniques</span>
      <h2>Iterative Prompt Refinement</h2>
      <p>Prompt engineering is iterative. Start with a basic prompt, evaluate results, identify gaps, and refine. Keep a log of prompts and results. Test variations systematically. What works depends on the model, task, and data. Consistent iteration yields the best results.</p>

      <h3>Evaluating and Improving Prompts</h3>
      <p>Create a feedback loop: write prompt, generate response, evaluate against criteria, identify failure modes, refine prompt. This systematic approach beats random tweaking. Document successful patterns for reuse.</p>

      <div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div>
      <pre><code><span class="cm"># Prompt iteration example</span>
<span class="va">prompts</span> <span class="op">=</span> {
    <span class="st">"v1_basic"</span>: <span class="st">"Summarize this article"</span>,
    <span class="st">"v2_specific"</span>: <span class="st">"Write a 100-word summary of this article for a technical audience"</span>,
    <span class="st">"v3_detailed"</span>: <span class="st">"""Summarize the article:
LENGTH: exactly 100 words
AUDIENCE: technical professionals familiar with ML
INCLUDE: key findings, methods, limitations
EXCLUDE: background information, author biography
FORMAT: 2-3 paragraphs"""</span>
}

<span class="cm"># Test each version</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> <span class="va">name</span>, <span class="va">prompt</span> <span class="kw">in</span> <span class="va">prompts</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">items</span>():
    <span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="va">openai</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">ChatCompletion</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">create</span>(
        <span class="va">model</span><span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
        <span class="va">messages</span><span class="op">=</span>[{<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>, <span class="st">"content"</span>: <span class="va">prompt</span>}]
    )
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="st">f"{name}: {len(response.choices[0].message.content.split())} words"</span>)
    <span class="bi">print</span>(<span class="va">response</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">choices</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>]<span class="op">.</span><span class="va">message</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="va">content</span><span class="op">.</span><span class="fn">[:100</span>])</span></code></pre>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="exercises">
      <span class="section-label">Practice</span>
      <h2>Exercises and Challenges</h2>

      <h3>Exercise 1: Prompt Comparison</h3>
      <p>Write 3 versions of a prompt for the same task: basic, good, and excellent. Test with an API and compare responses. Document which version performs best and why.</p>

      <h3>Exercise 2: Structured Prompting</h3>
      <p>Use the RCFC framework to write a prompt for a complex task (data analysis, code review, content generation). Evaluate whether the structure improved response quality.</p>

      <h3>Exercise 3: Error Handling</h3>
      <p>Identify a task where models often make mistakes. Design prompts with explicit error prevention. Test iterations to reduce failure rate.</p>
    </section>

